{
  "dom_id": "step-t4834",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "How do we solve an equation with a variable raised to an odd exponent, such as\n\n$$\nx^3 = 125?\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by taking the $3$rd root of each side and solving for $x.$ \n\nSince $n=3$ is odd, there will be only one real solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{{x}^{3}} & =\\sqrt[3]{125} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{{5}^{3}} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5.$\n\nIn general, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n*When $n$ is odd, and we take the $n$th root of both sides of $x^n = a,$ we get only one solution:* \n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt[n]{a}\n$$",
